# Environment variables the web app reads. These are the only three (every one starts with
# VITE_, which Vite copies into the browser bundle at build time).
#
# For local development: cp .env.example .env.local, and fill in the two Supabase values.
#
# EVERYTHING here ends up in the browser, so nothing here may be a secret. The Supabase
# values are public by design (the publishable key is made to be embedded in client apps;
# row-level security is what protects the data). NEVER put the service-role key here.

# --- REQUIRED: Supabase (the same project the API uses) ---
# Hosted project: dashboard -> Project Settings -> API (the project URL and the
# publishable key, `sb_publishable_...`).
# Local stack (`supabase start`): run `supabase status -o env` in the repository root; the
# URL is API_URL (http://127.0.0.1:54321) and the key is PUBLISHABLE_KEY.
# The app throws at start-up without both.
VITE_SUPABASE_URL=https://your-project-ref.supabase.co
VITE_SUPABASE_PUBLISHABLE_KEY=sb_publishable_...

# --- The API's origin ---
# LEAVE UNSET for local development. Requests then go to /api, and Vite's dev proxy
# (vite.config.ts) forwards them to the API on http://localhost:8012, with the /api prefix
# stripped. A blank value counts as unset; a trailing slash on a value is dropped.
#
# REQUIRED for any `npm run build`: a built bundle has no proxy, so the SPA and the API are
# different origins and the bundle must be told where the API is. The build refuses to run
# (web/scripts/apiBaseGuard.ts) unless the value is https://host[/path] -- no credentials,
# no query string or fragment, and not a local address (localhost, 127.0.0.1, 0.0.0.0, ::1,
# *.localhost, *.local) -- because every request carries the user's session token. Pass it
# in the shell rather than in this file, so a leftover .env.local cannot leak into a
# bundle you meant to deploy:
#
#   VITE_API_BASE_URL=https://api.example.com npm run build
#
# The API answers cross-origin requests from any origin (it authenticates with a bearer
# token, never a cookie), so the API itself needs no CORS setting for a deployed SPA.
# VITE_API_BASE_URL=https://api.example.com
